    "doc_id": "2024-12-18-015-en",
    "num": "15",
    "date_str": "2024-12-18",
    "description": "Continuation of the implementation of the Special Commodity Levy Act, No.48 of 2007",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12943",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-12-18 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- At the Cabinet meeting held on 2024\u201103\u201125, it was decided to repeal the Special Commodity Levy Act, No.48 of 2007, where provisions have made for the importation of 64 goods including essential food items under the Special Commodity Levy executed as a single composite levy. However, it has been observed that it is appropriate to further continue with the Special Commodity Levy Act, No.48 of 2007, taking into account the likely enforcement of other taxes including Value Added Tax with the repealing of the said Act, on the goods on which the Special Commodity Levy was applicable and the consequent increase of market price of essential goods, and the likely adverse impact on the government policy on facilitating the local farmers. As such, the proposal made by the Hon. President, in his capacity as the Minister of Finance, Planning and Economic Development, not to implement the Cabinet decision dated 2024\u201103\u201125 to repeal the relevant Tax and to continue to enforce the Special Commodity Levy component on goods falling under the Special Commodity Levy Act, No.48 of 2007, with effect from 2025\u201101\u201101, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2024-12-18-014-en",
    "num": "14",
    "date_str": "2024-12-18",
    "description": "Importation of salt to address the shortage of salt",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12942",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-12-18 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- Salt production in Sri Lanka is sufficient to meet the household and industrial demand of the country and the salt harvest depends on weather conditions. It has been observed that there might be a shortage of salt supply  in the domestic market for household and industrial needs, in the first quarter of 2025, owing to the prevailed inclement weather in the country. As a remedy, the salt manufacturers have requested permission to import up to a maximum of 30,000 metric tons of raw non-iodized salt at the prevailing tariff rate for processing and supply to the market. Accordingly, the joint proposal made by the Minister of Trade, Commerce, Food Security and Cooperative Development and the Minister of Industry and Entrepreneurship Development to import raw non-iodized salt, subject to a maximum of 30,000 metric tons, by the Sri Lanka State Trading (General) Corporation Ltd., on or before 2025\u201101\u201131 and supply the same to the market through local salt manufacturers, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2024-12-18-013-en",
    "num": "13",
    "date_str": "2024-12-18",
    "description": "Amendments to the Trusts Ordinance",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12941",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-12-18 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- The Financial Action Task Force which is an intergovernmental organization working for developing policies to combat money laundering, has stated the need to amend the provisions of the Trusts Ordinance in order to secure a favorable position in the upcoming Mutual Evaluation of Sri Lanka to be conducted in 2025. The Financial Intelligence Unit of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka and the Registrar General's Department have submitted the proposed amendments for the purpose. Accordingly, the proposal made by the Minister of Justice and National Integration to amend the Trusts Ordinance incorporating the said proposed amendments, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
