  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2024-06-03-003-en",
    "num": "3",
    "date_str": "2024-06-03",
    "description": "Reforming the Education Administration System for Drastic Transformative Change in Education",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12568",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-06-03 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- The National Education Policy Framework 2023-2033 prepared in accordance with the vision of \"a fully developed Sri Lanka by the year 2048\" aiming to provide equal opportunities and quality education to each and every student in the country, has presented facts about the transformative change that should take place in education. Accordingly, the need for a new transformation in the organizational structure, curriculum and evaluation method has been identified to ensure a long-term learning methodology. Accordingly Cabinet approved the proposal made by the Minister of Education for taking necessary action as follows, with a view to establishing an appropriate administrative structure for the efficient and effective implementation of the overall educational reforms, based on the recommendations of the Cabinet Sub-Committee appointed under the Chairmanship of the Hon. President regarding the preparation of the National Education Policy Framework:\n\n*\tClassification of schools in compliance with the international standards.\n\n*\tEstablishment of 09 Educational Reform Units in the 09 provinces.\n\n*\tIncreasing the number of Zonal Education Offices from 100 to 122 in stages, based on a scientific and priority order.\n\n*\tEstablishment of 350 School Boards also including the existing 314 Divisional Education Offices aiming to ensure the school supervision, \tmonitoring teachers and verifying the validity of school-based evaluations.\n\n*\tDesignating one or more selected secondary schools as leading schools and inclusion of the entire school system into 1,220 school clusters of which a single cluster school contains approximately 8\u201112 numbers of schools.\n\n*\tRecruitment of required officers for the Sri Lanka Education Administrative Service and Teacher Advisors' Service."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2024-06-03-002-en",
    "num": "2",
    "date_str": "2024-06-03",
    "description": "Implementation of the Article 6 of the Paris Agreement in Sri Lanka",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12567",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-06-03 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- The Paris Agreement, which is a legally binding international treaty on climate change, has been adopted by 196 parties at the 21st Conference of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change (COP21) held in 2015 in Paris, and Sri Lanka has signed the said Agreement in 2016. The main objective of the Paris Agreement is to maintain the increase in the global average temperature to well below 02 degrees Celsius above the pre-industrial levels and to limit the temperature increase to 1.5 degrees Celsius above the pre-industrial levels. As per the provisions of the said Agreement, the Parties should implement \"Nationally Determined Contributions (NDCs)\", for which 02 market mechanisms have been introduced. It paves the way for the countries to bilaterally exchange the outcomes of greenhouse gas mitigation, to report such exchanges of greenhouse gas and to utilize them to achieve the Nationally Determined Contributions of the partner countries. Further, it has been introduced a new international carbon crediting mechanism through this Agreement. Under that situation, Sri Lanka is preparing Carbon Market Strategy and Guiding Principles with the technical support of the Regional Collaboration Centre, Dubai of the United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change, and it is expected to complete the work by the end of this year. In the meantime, various project developers have submitted project proposals related to the main sectors of electricity, industry, waste management, transport, agriculture and forestry identified by Sri Lanka to mitigate greenhouse gas emissions. The proposal made by the Hon. President in his capacity as the Minister of Environment to appoint a Committee chaired by the Secretary, Ministry of Environment to evaluate the said project proposals and reach decisions until the Carbon Market Strategy and Guiding Principles are implemented, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_cabinet_decisions",
    "doc_id": "2024-06-03-001-en",
    "num": "1",
    "date_str": "2024-06-03",
    "description": "Removal of the requirement of Import Control License for importation of selected edible grains",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.cabinetoffice.gov.lk/cab/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=16&Itemid=49&lang=en&dID=12566",
    "lang": "en",
    "decision_details_title": "Press briefing of Cabinet Decision taken on 2024-06-03 (Subject to Confirmation at the next Meeting)",
    "decision_details_body": "- As of 2024\u201105\u201101, selected edible grains and commodities which have been classified under 126 HS Codes, have been subjected to the Import Control License whereas issuance of import control license relating to 53 HS Codes has been suspended as of now. In this backdrop, it has been found appropriate to remove the requirement of Import Control License for importation of grains in order to achieve the multiple objectives of eliminating black-market operation, illicit importation of grains, preventing the inflated retail price in an unfair and unjust manner and ensuring affordable price to the consumer and protection of domestic production by imposition of appropriate rate of duties and quarantine procedures. Accordingly, taking into account the recommendations made by a Committee chaired by the Secretary to the President and comprising of the Members of the Governor of the Central Bank of Sri Lanka; Secretary to the General Treasury and the Senior Economic Advisor to the President, the proposal made by the Hon. President, in his capacity as the Minister of Finance, Economic Stabilization and National Policies to remove the requirement of Import Control License for importation of green gram, black gram, cow peas, kurakkan, flour of soya beans and ground nuts while taking necessary measures so as to protect the local manufacturers, subject to  submission of the National Plant Quarantine Permit of the National Plant Quarantine Service and based on the recommendations of the Ministry of Agriculture and Plantation Industries, was approved by the Cabinet."
  },
